  "constraintReading": "len(height) can reach 2·10⁴, so scanning both sides of every bar is about 4·10⁸ steps: O(n²) is too slow in Python. O(n) means each bar learns its two walls from running maximums. The O(1) space target rules out storing a maximum for every index, which is what two pointers save: they only ever need the running maximum on the lower side.",
  "hints": {
    "clarify": "How much water sits over one bar? Try [3, 0, 2] by hand: over the 0, the water rises to 2, the lower of the tallest bar to its left (3) and to its right (2), so it holds 2. Over the 2, the tallest bar to the right is the 2 itself, so nothing settles there. The total is 2. The end bars never hold water: it spills off.",
    "approach": "Two pointers from opposite ends, each with a running maximum. Water over a bar is set by the lower of its two walls. If `height[l]` is lower than `height[r]`, the right side has a bar at least as tall as anything on the left so far, so the left wall decides: bar l holds `left_max − height[l]`, where `left_max` is the tallest bar seen from the left, bar l included. Add that and move l right. Otherwise do the same on the right side and move r left.",
    "whyNot": "Scanning left and right of every bar for its walls is O(n²). Precomputing the tallest bar to the left and to the right of every index fixes the time, O(n), but takes two arrays of n numbers. A monotonic stack also runs in O(n) time with O(n) space. Two pointers reach O(1) space, because the lower side is always the one that decides.",
    "slots": {
      "setup": "`l` on the first bar, `r` on the last, `left_max` and `right_max` at 0, and the water total at 0.",
      "loop": "Keep going while `l` is left of `r`: every bar between them still needs its water counted.",
      "update": "Work on the side with the lower bar. Raise that side's running maximum to its current bar if the bar is taller, then move that pointer inward.",
      "record": "Before moving, add that side's maximum minus the current bar's height to the total. It is 0 when the bar is the tallest so far on its side.",
      "return": "Return the total once the pointers meet; the bar where they meet is the tallest, and holds no water."
    }
  },
  "solution": {
    "code": "class Solution:\n    def trap(self, height: list[int]) -> int:\n        l, r = 0, len(height) - 1\n        left_max = right_max = 0\n        water = 0\n        while l < r:\n            if height[l] < height[r]:\n                left_max = max(left_max, height[l])  # viz:left\n                water += left_max - height[l]\n                l += 1\n            else:\n                right_max = max(right_max, height[r])  # viz:right\n                water += right_max - height[r]\n                r -= 1\n        return water  # viz:done\n",
    "explanation": "Each step moves one pointer one bar inward, so the loop runs n − 1 times: O(n) time. Only two pointers, two running maximums and the total are stored: O(1) space. Why the lower side is safe: when `height[l] < height[r]`, the right side already has a bar taller than `height[l]`, and `left_max` has never seen a bar as tall as `height[r]` (or the pointer would have stopped moving), so the left wall is the lower of the two walls of bar l.",
    "toolkit": []
  },
